"To evaluate \\(\\lfloor 6.7 \\rfloor + \\lfloor -6.7 \\rfloor\\), we need to understand the floor function, denoted by \\(\\lfloor x \\rfloor\\), which gives the greatest integer less than or equal to \\(x\\).\n\n1. Calculate \\(\\lfloor 6.7 \\rfloor\\):\n   - The greatest integer less than or equal to 6.7 is 6.\n   - Therefore, \\(\\lfloor 6.7 \\rfloor = 6\\).\n\n2. Calculate \\(\\lfloor -6.7 \\rfloor\\):\n   - The greatest integer less than or equal to -6.7 is -7.\n   - Therefore, \\(\\lfloor -6.7 \\rfloor = -7\\).\n\n3. Add the two results together:\n   \\[\n   \\lfloor 6.7 \\rfloor + \\lfloor -6.7 \\rfloor = 6 + (-7)\n   \\]\n   Simplifying this:\n   \\[\n   6 - 7 = -1\n   \\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(\\lfloor 6.7 \\rfloor + \\lfloor -6.7 \\rfloor\\) is \\(\\boxed{-1}\\)."
"The midpoint of a line segment between two points \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) is given by \\(\\left( \\frac{x_1 + x_2}{2}, \\frac{y_1 + y_2}{2} \\right)\\). Given that the midpoint is \\((3, -5)\\), we can set up the following equations:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x + (-9)}{2} = 3 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\frac{y + 1}{2} = -5\n\\]\n\nSolving the first equation for \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x - 9}{2} = 3 \\implies x - 9 = 6 \\implies x = 15\n\\]\n\nSolving the second equation for \\(y\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{y + 1}{2} = -5 \\implies y + 1 = -10 \\implies y = -11\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the coordinates \\((x, y)\\) are \\((15, -11)\\).\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{(15, -11)}\\)."
"We know that for a quadratic equation $ax^2+bx+c=0$, the sum of the roots is $\\frac $-}{a}$ and the product of the roots is $\\frac{c}{a}$. Therefore, for the given equation $2x^2-10x+5=0$, we have:\n\n$$a+b = \\frac{10}{2} = 5$$\n$$ab = \\frac{5}{2} = \\frac{5}{2}$$\n\nWe need to find the value of $(2a-3)(4b-6)$. Expanding this expression, we get:\n\n$$(2a-3)(4b-6) = 8ab - 12a - 12b + 18$$\n\nSubstituting the values of $a+b$ and $ab$, we get:\n\n$$(2a-3)(4b-6) = 8(\\frac{5}{2}) - 12(5) + 18 = 20 - 60 + 18 = \\boxed{-22}$$ \n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $-22$. I hope it is correct."
