"To determine the degree of the polynomial \\((ax^6 + bx^8 + cx^3 + d)(x^5 + ex^4 + f)(x^2 + gx + h)\\), we need to consider the highest degree term in each factor and then sum these degrees.\n\n1. The highest degree term in \\(ax^6 + bx^8 + cx^3 + d\\) is \\(bx^8\\). Therefore, the degree of this polynomial is 8.\n\n2. The highest degree term in \\(x^5 + ex^4 + f\\) is \\(x^5\\). Therefore, the degree of this polynomial is 5.\n\n3. The highest degree term in \\(x^2 + gx + h\\) is \\(x^2\\). Therefore, the degree of this polynomial is 2.\n\nWhen multiplying these polynomials, the degree of the resulting polynomial is the sum of the degrees of the individual polynomials. Therefore, we add the degrees of the polynomials:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Degree of the polynomial} = 8 + 5 + 2 = 15\n\\]\n\nThus, the degree of the polynomial is \\(\\boxed{15}\\)."
