"To find the sum of the squares of the solutions to the quadratic equation \\(2x^2 + 4x - 1 = 0\\), we can use the formula for the sum of the squares of the roots of a quadratic equation \\(ax^2 + bx + c = 0\\):\n\n\\[\n\\text{Sum of squares} = \\left(-\\frac{b}{a}\\right)^2 - 2\\left(\\frac{c}{a}\\right)\n\\]\n\nFor the given equation \\(2x^2 + 4x - 1 = 0\\), we have \\(a = 2\\), \\(b = 4\\), and \\(c = -1\\). Plugging these values into the formula, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Sum of squares} = \\left(-\\frac{4}{2}\\right)^2 - 2\\left(\\frac{-1}{2}\\right)\n\\]\n\nSimplifying this, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Sum of squares} = (-2)^2 - 2(-1) = 4 + 1 = 5\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of the squares of the solutions is \\(\\boxed{5}\\)."
