"To find the equation of the oblique asymptote of the graph of \\(\\frac{2x^2+x-13}{2x-5}\\), we need to perform polynomial long division on the numerator by the denominator.\n\n1. Divide the leading term of the numerator by the leading term of the denominator:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{2x^2}{2x} = x\n   \\]\n\n2. Multiply \\(x\\) by the entire denominator \\(2x - 5\\):\n   \\[\n   x \\cdot (2x - 5) = 2x^2 - 5x\n   \\]\n\n3. Subtract this result from the original numerator:\n   \\[\n   (2x^2 + x - 13) - (2x^2 - 5x) = 6x - 13\n   \\]\n\nThe quotient of the division is \\(x + 3\\).\n\nTherefore, the equation of the oblique asymptote is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{y = x + 3}\n\\]\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(y = x + 3\\). I hope it is correct."
