"To find out how many students played both hockey and baseball, we can use the principle of inclusion and exclusion. Let's denote the number of students who played hockey as \\( H \\), the number of students who played baseball as \\( B \\), and the number of students who played both hockey and baseball as \\( N \\).\n\nGiven:\n- \\( H = 33 \\)\n- \\( B = 24 \\)\n- \\(8\\) students played neither hockey nor baseball, so the number of students who played at least one of the sports is \\( 50 - 8 = 42 \\).\n\nUsing the principle of inclusion and exclusion, the number of students who played at least one of the sports is given by:\n\\[ |H \\cup B| = H + B - N \\]\n\nSubstituting the known values:\n\\[ 42 = 33 + 24 - N \\]\n\nSolving for \\( N \\):\n\\[ 42 = 57 - N \\]\n\\[ N = 15 \\]  \\[ N = 15 \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of students who played both hockey and baseball is \\(\\boxed{15}\\)."
