"To determine how much milk will be left in the bucket when Sam gets home, we need to calculate the amount of milk remaining after each mile he walks.\n\n1. **Initial amount of milk:**\n   Sam starts with 2 gallons of milk.\n\n2. **First mile:**\n   After the first mile, the amount of milk remaining is \\(\\frac{2}{3}\\) of the initial amount.\n   \\[\n   \\frac{2}{3} \\times 2 = \\frac{4}{3} \\text{ gallons}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Second mile:**\n   After the second mile, the amount of milk remaining is \\(\\frac{2}{3}\\) of the amount after the first mile.\n   \\[\n   \\frac{2}{3} \\times \\frac{4}{3} = \\frac{8}{9} \\text{ gallons}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the amount of milk left in the bucket when Sam gets home is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{8}{9} \\text{ gallons}}\n\\]\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(\\frac{8}{9}\\). I hope it is correct."
