"To determine the number of pieces when the cube of cheese is cut along the planes \\( x = y \\), \\( y = z \\), and \\( z = x \\), we need to analyze the effect of these cuts on the cube.\n\n1. **Cutting along \\( x = y \\)**:\n   - This cut divides the cube into two vertical layers.\n\n2. **Cutting along \\( y = z \\)**:\n   - This cut divides the cube into two horizontal layers.\n\n3. **Cutting along \\( z = x \\)**:\n   - This cut divides the cube into two diagonal layers.\n\nWhen these cuts are made, the cube is divided into smaller rectangular pieces. Each piece is a face of the original cube, but since the cuts are made, each face is divided into two smaller rectangles.\n\nTo find the total number of pieces, we multiply the number of pieces along each dimension:\n\\[\n\\text{Number of layers} \\times \\text{Number of layers} \\times \\text{Number of layers} = 2 \\times 2 \\times 2 = 8\n\\]\n\nThus, the total number of pieces is \\(\\boxed{8}\\)."
"To determine the smallest positive integer \\( n \\) such that \\((\\cos 84^\\circ + i \\sin 84^\\circ)^n\\) is a real number, we need to use the properties of complex numbers and Euler's formula.\n\nFirst, recall Euler's formula:\n\\[ e^{i\\theta} = \\cos \\theta + i \\sin \\theta. \\]\n\nThus,\n\\[ \\cos 84^\\circ + i \\sin 84^\\circ = e^{i 84^\\circ}. \\]\n\nWe need to find the smallest \\( n \\) such that \\( (e^{i 84^\\circ})^n = e^{i \\cdot 84^\\circ \\cdot n} \\) is a real number. This occurs when the exponent is a multiple of \\( 360^\\circ \\).\n\nTherefore, we need:\n\\[ 84^\\circ \\cdot n = 360^\\circ. \\]\n\nSolving for \\( n \\):\n\\[ 84 = 360 \\implies n = 5. \\]\n\nThus, the smallest positive integer \\( n \\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{5}. \\]"
"We know that the determinant of a 3x3 matrix is given by the formula:\n\n\\[\\begin{vmatrix} a & b & c \\\\ d & e & f \\\\ g & h & i \\\\ j & k & l \\end{vmatrix} = a(ei - fh) - b(dl - fg) + c(dh - eg).\\]\n\nFor the given matrix, we have:\n\n\\[\\begin{vmatrix} a & b & c \\\\ 2d + 5g & 2e + 5h & 2f + 5i \\\\ -g & -h & -i \\end{vmatrix} = a((2e + 5h)(-i) - (2f + 5i)(-h)) - b((2d + 5g)(-i) - (2f + 5i)(-g)) + c((2d + 5g)(-h) - (2e + 5h)(-g)).\\]\n\nSimplifying this expression, we can find that the determinant of the given matrix is $\\boxed{14}$.\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $14$. I hope it is correct."
"To find the point the curve passes through at \\( t = 2 \\), we need to substitute \\( t = 2 \\) into the parameterized equations \\((x, y) = (t^3 + 7, -3t^2 - 6t - 5)\\).\n\nFirst, calculate \\( x \\):\n\\[\nx = t^3 + 7\n\\]\nSubstitute \\( t = 2 \\):\n\\[\nx = 2^3 + 7 = 8 + 7 = 15\n\\]\n\nNext, calculate \\( y \\):\n\\[\ny = -3t^2 - 6t - 5\n\\]\nSubstitute \\( t = 2 \\):\n\\[\ny = -3(2)^2 - 6(2) - 5 = -3(4) - 12 - 5 = -12 - 12 - 5 = -29\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the point the curve passes through at \\( t = 2 \\) is \\(\\boxed{(15, -29)}\\).\n\nThe final answer is \\((15, -29)\\). I hope it is correct."
"To add the vectors \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -11 \\\\ 10 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we add their corresponding components:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -11 \\\\ 10 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 5 + (-11) \\\\ -4 + 10 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nCalculating each component:\n\n\\[\n5 + (-11) = 5 - 11 = -6\n\\]\n\\[\n-4 + 10 = 6\n\\]\n\nSo, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -11 \\\\ 10 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -6 \\\\ 6 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -6 \\\\ 6 \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -6 \\\\ 6 \\end{pmatrix}\\). I hope it is correct."
"To find the complex number that the translation takes \\(-6\\) to, we first need to determine the vector vector of the translation. The translation takes the complex number \\(5 + 5i\\) to \\(6 + 8i\\). We can find the vector by subtracting the original complex number from the translated complex number:\n\n\\[\n(6 + 8i) - (5 + 5i) = 6 + 8i - 5 - 5i = 1 + 3i\n\\]\n\nSo, the translation vector is \\(1 + 3i\\).\n\nNext, we apply this translation vector to the complex number \\(-6\\):\n\n\\[\n(-6 + 0i) + (1 + 3i) = -6 + 0i + 1 + 3i = -5 + 3i\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the complex number that the translation takes \\(-6\\) to is \\(\\boxed{-5 + 3i}\\)."
