"To find the period of the function \\( y = \\cos x + \\cos 2x + \\cos 3x \\), we need to consider the periods of the individual cosine functions and find the least common multiple (LCM) of these periods.\n\nThe period of a cosine function \\( \\cos(kx) \\) is given by \\( \\frac{2\\pi}{k} \\).\n\n1. The period of \\( \\cos x \\) is \\( \\frac{2\\pi}{1} = 2\\pi \\).\n2. The period of \\( \\cos 2x \\) is \\( \\frac{2\\pi}{2} = \\pi \\).\n3. The period of \\( \\cos 3x \\) is \\( \\frac{2\\pi}{3} = \\frac{2\\pi}{3} \\).\n\nThe LCM of these periods is \\( 2\\pi \\), \\( \\pi \\), and \\( \\frac{2\\pi}{3} \\). The least common multiple of these three values is \\( 2\\pi \\).\n\nTherefore, the period of the function \\( y = \\cos x + \\cos 2x + \\cos 3x \\) is \\(\\boxed{2\\pi}\\)."
